"[2511.07581v1] Think Before You Retrieve: Learning Test-Time Adaptive Search with Small Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07581v1)\nciteturn1912view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1909view1\",\"lineno\":44}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Think Before You Retrieve: Learning Test-Time Adaptive Search with Small Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Supriti Vijay , cite6†Aman Priyanshu , cite7†Anu Vellore , cite8†Baturay Saglam , cite9†Amin Karbasi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Think Before You Retrieve: Learning Test-Time Adaptive Search with Small Language Models, by Supriti Vijay and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Effective information retrieval requires reasoning over partial evidence and refining strategies as information emerges. Yet current approaches fall short: neural retrievers lack reasoning capabilities, large language models (LLMs) provide semantic depth but at prohibitive cost, and query rewriting or decomposition limits improvement to static transformations.\nL17: As a result, existing methods fail to capture the iterative dynamics of exploration, feedback, and revision that complex user queries demand. We introduce Orion, a training framework that enables compact models (350M-1.2B parameters) to perform iterative retrieval through learned search strategies.\nL18: Orion combines: (1) synthetic trajectory generation and supervised fine-tuning to encourage diverse exploration patterns in models, (2) reinforcement learning (RL) that rewards effective query refinement and backtracking behaviors, and (3) inference-time beam search algorithms that exploit the self-reflection capabilities learned during RL. Despite using only 3% of the training data available, our 1.2B model achieves 77.6% success on SciFact (vs. 72.6% for prior retrievers), 25.2% on BRIGHT (vs.\nL19: 22.1%), 63.2% on NFCorpus (vs. 57.8%), and remains competitive on FEVER, HotpotQA, and MSMarco. It outperforms retrievers up to 200-400x larger on five of six benchmarks. These findings suggest that retrieval performance can emerge from learned strategies, not just model scale, when models are trained to search, reflect, and revise.\nL20: Comments:  | 37 images, 7 figures, and 15 tables\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Information Retrieval (cs.IR)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.07581 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.07581v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.07581†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Supriti Vijay [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 10 Nov 2025 19:49:55 UTC (485 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Think Before You Retrieve: Learning Test-Time Adaptive Search with Small Language Models, by Supriti Vijay and 4 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.AI\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite23†cs L52: cite24†cs.CL L53: cite25†cs.IR L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * ci