\"turn1909view2\",\"lineno\":43}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:ResearchRubrics: A Benchmark of Prompts and Rubrics For Evaluating Deep Research Agents\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Manasi Sharma , cite6†Chen Bo Calvin Zhang , cite7†Chaithanya Bandi , cite8†Clinton Wang , cite9†Ankit Aich , cite10†Huy Nghiem , cite11†Tahseen Rabbani , cite12†Ye Htet , cite13†Brian Jang , cite14†Sumana Basu , cite15†Aishwarya Balwani , cite16†Denis Peskoff , cite17†Marcos Ayestaran , cite18†Sean M. Hendryx , cite19†Brad Kenstler , cite20†Bing Liu L12: View a PDF of the paper titled ResearchRubrics: A Benchmark of Prompts and Rubrics For Evaluating Deep Research Agents, by Manasi Sharma and 15 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite21†View PDF cite22†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:Deep Research (DR) is an emerging agent application that leverages large language models (LLMs) to address open-ended queries. It requires the integration of several capabilities, including multi-step reasoning, cross-document synthesis, and the generation of evidence-backed, long-form answers. Evaluating DR remains challenging because responses are lengthy and diverse, admit many valid solutions, and often depend on dynamic information sources.\nL16: We introduce ResearchRubrics, a standardized benchmark for DR built with over 2,800+ hours of human labor that pairs realistic, domain-diverse prompts with 2,500+ expert-written, fine-grained rubrics to assess factual grounding, reasoning soundness, and clarity. We also propose a new complexity framework for categorizing DR tasks along three axes: conceptual breadth, logical nesting, and exploration.\nL17: In addition, we develop human and model-based evaluation protocols that measure rubric adherence for DR agents. We evaluate several state-of-the-art DR systems and find that even leading agents like Gemini's DR and OpenAI's DR achieve under 68% average compliance with our rubrics, primarily due to missed implicit context and inadequate reasoning about retrieved information.\nL18: Our results highlight the need for robust, scalable assessment of deep research capabilities, to which end we release ResearchRubrics(including all prompts, rubrics, and evaluation code) to facilitate progress toward well-justified research assistants.\nL19: Comments:  | 27 pages, 21 figures, pre-print\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite23†arXiv:2511.07685 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite24†arXiv:2511.07685v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite25†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.07685†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Chen Bo Calvin Zhang [cite26†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Mon, 10 Nov 2025 23:07:14 UTC (3,338 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled ResearchRubrics: A Benchmark of Prompts and Rubrics For Evaluating Deep Research Agents, by Manasi Sharma and 15 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite21†View PDF L36:   * cite22†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite27†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite28†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.AI\nL43: \nL44: cite29†< prev |   cite30†next > L45: \nL46: cite31†new | cite32†recent | cite33†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite34†cs L51: cite35†cs.CL L52: cite36†cs.LG L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite37†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite38†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com 